-- title:
--   Raynaud normal-form model of an inertia-simple step
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be an odd prime and let $H$ be a commutative ring carrying a cocommutative Hopf algebra structure over the subring $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)} \subset \mathbb{Q}$ of rationals whose denominator is coprime to $p$, finite and flat as a $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$-module, and suppose every element of the convolution monoid $\mathrm{WithConv}$ of $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$-algebra maps $H \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ satisfies $f^p = 1$. Let $P$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a non-unit of $P$, and write $I_P$ for $P.\mathrm{inertiaSubgroupIn}\ \mathbb{Q}$, the image in $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ of the inertia subgroup of the decomposition subgroup of $P$. Let $K \le K'$ be submonoids of the convolution monoid, each stable in the sense that whenever $\sigma \in I_P$, $f$ lies in the submonoid and $g$ satisfies $g(h) = \sigma(f(h))$ for all $h \in H$, then $g$ lies in it too; assume every such stable submonoid $S$ with $K \le S \le K'$ equals $K$ or $K'$, and that $\mathrm{card}\,K' = p^s\,\mathrm{card}\,K$ for some $s \ne 0$. The conclusion asserts the existence of: a commutative ring $R'$ with an algebra map to $P$; a finite field $F$; a commutative ring $B$ with a Hopf $R'$-algebra structure; an $F$-vector space structure $fv$ on $B$ in Raynaud's sense, i.e. a map $\mathrm{act} : F \to (B \to_{\mathrm{alg},\mathrm{coalg}} B)$ with $\mathrm{act}\,1 = \mathrm{id}$, $\mathrm{act}(ab) = \mathrm{act}\,a \circ \mathrm{act}\,b$, $\mathrm{act}\,0$ the convolution unit and $\mathrm{act}(a+b)$ the convolution product of $\mathrm{act}\,a$ and $\mathrm{act}\,b$; a monoid homomorphism $\chi : F^\times \to R'^\times$; a ring homomorphism $\iota_0$ from $F$ to the residue field of $P$; families $X_i \in B$ and $\delta_i \in R'$ indexed by $\mathrm{Fin}\,s$; and a map $q$ from the convolution monoid to the set of $R'$-algebra maps $B \to P$, such that $p$ is irreducible in $R'$; every $\sigma \in I_P$ fixes the image of $R'$ in $P$ pointwise; $|F| = p^s$; the residue of $\chi(l)$ in $P$ is $\iota_0(l)$ for all $l \in F^\times$; $\mathrm{act}(l)(X_i) = \chi^{p^i}(l)\,X_i$; $X_i^p = \delta_i X_{i+1}$ with indices read cyclically; $\delta_i \mid p$; the counit kills each $X_i$; the $R'$-subalgebra generated by the $X_i$ is all of $B$; there are exactly $p^s$ $R'$-algebra maps $B \to P$; $q$ is multiplicative on $K'$ after passing to the convolution monoid of such maps; for $f \in K'$, $q(f)$ is the convolution unit exactly when $f \in K$; every $R'$-algebra map $B \to P$ is $q(f)$ for some $f \in K'$; and $q$ is $I_P$-equivariant, in the sense that if $\sigma \in I_P$, $f \in K'$ and $g(h) = \sigma(f(h))$ for all $h \in H$, then $q(g)(x) = \sigma(q(f)(x))$ in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ for all $x \in B$.
--
--   This is the Raynaud structure theory of finite flat $F$-vector space schemes, applied to one inertia-simple step $K'/K$ of the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of a finite flat group scheme over $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$ killed by $p$: the step is modelled by a Hopf order $B$ in normal form $X_i^p = \delta_i X_{i+1}$ whose points parametrise $K'/K$ equivariantly for inertia at $p$. It feeds the extraction of an additive eigenfunctional and the computation of the action of inertia through powers of the tame character, used in the analysis of the local behaviour at $p$ of the residual representation.
